[Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  hi i'm stephanie wong a developer  advocate here at google and today i'm  going to talk to you about pi  well  actually we're talking about the  mathematical number here but let's use  this apple pie to illustrate the new  world record of pi calculation we just  set  this little bit here represents 1  trillion digits  well using google cloud's compute engine  we calculated 100 trillion digits of pi  adding more than 37 trillion digits to  the previous record set in 2021. another  way to think about it if you had 100  trillion of these toy bricks you could  build the entire great wall of china and  still have 39 trillion bricks to spare  of course you might be asking why  calculate trillions of digits of pi when  you only need dozens of digits for  scientific purposes  well calculating pi has been one of the  best ways to measure human progress in  calculations over the last several  decades  in 1949 one of the first electronic  computers eniac calculated  2037 decimal places of pi  since then scientists and engineers all  around the world have worked to get to  as many digits as possible the jump from  2000 to 100 trillion shows how much  faster our computers and algorithms have  become over the last several decades  we calculated pi twice using google  cloud the first time was in 2019 when we  hit 31.4 trillion decimals  this year we calculated 100 trillion  decimals more than three times our  previous world record  this means our cloud infrastructure such  as virtual machines network and storage  improved to handle even more data  and even though we calculated three  times more digits we only spent 157 days  or 30 percent more time than that 121  days it took to get to 31.4 trillion  digits  so  how did we run the numbers exactly  we used a program called y cruncher  developed by alexander yi  it runs on a single virtual machine just  like a web server  it's just extremely compute storage and  memory intensive  so we used an n2 high mem128 compute  engine instance with 128 vcpus and  864 gigabytes of memory it is one of the  largest virtual machines you can use on  google cloud  y cruncher uses both memory and disks to  store data during the calculation and  100 trillion digits requires about 515  terabytes of storage the maximum  capacity you can attach to a single vm  is 257 terabytes so we needed to get a  little creative to add more disks  we used a storage protocol called iscsi  to mount additional disks to a virtual  machine  with iscsi you can use a remote drive  just like your local ssd  we had a total capacity of 663 terabytes  spanning across 32 n2 high cpu 16 vms  each with two 10.4 terabyte persistent  disks  iscsi uses a standard tcpip protocol to  communicate with storage nodes the n2  highmem128 compute node has 100 gigabits  per second bandwidth up from 16 gigabits  per second in 2019 and this increase  helped run the calculation much faster  than the last time  we also used a new type of persistent  disk called balanced pd  balance pd offers up to 1200 megabytes  per second read and write throughput and  15 to 80 000 iops for about sixty  percent of the cost of ssdpds  this storage profile is a sweet spot for  y cruncher which needs high throughput  and medium iops this reduced the overall  storage cost significantly  we tried many different parameters  before coming up with this particular  configuration  we used terraform to manage virtual  machines so that we could try different  configurations quickly  we also developed a small ruby script to  test why cruncher with various  parameters too which helped us test  about a hundred different combinations  of settings in about a week  the final design was about twice as fast  as our first design which means the  calculation could have taken twice as  long without all the measurements and  optimizations  we also wrote a few shell scripts to  automate backups and restores during the  calculation  backups are automatically taken every  two days as persistent as snapshots  unlike regular disk copies pd snapshots  happen in the background which means the  calculation needs to pause for a few  seconds and resume quickly while the  compute engine infrastructure copies the  data blocks asynchronously  this feature minimizes the downtime for  the backups letting us run the script as  often as needed without performance  penalty  backups are only useful though when you  can actually restore data from them so  we tested the resource script and made  sure that we were able to restart from  one of the backups  we deleted and recreated the virtual  machines as if all data were lost  this practice gave us the confidence and  the preparations we made because we knew  the calculations would take months  instead of days the scripts we used are  all available on github so check out the  link in the description if you want to  learn more about automation after  running the program for five months we  got the result  but it's not the end of the story there  is no way of knowing whether the answer  was correct until we verified it with  another algorithm at the end  and this was one of the scariest moments  during the project because the time you  spent might end up with some random  numbers instead of pi  fortunately the answers did match and  the results were valid  the total amount of data we read and  wrote was 82 petabytes and the  infrastructure got every bit correct  for reference that's almost 820 000  2-hour 4k movies  i think this is a good example of the  reliability of the cloud infrastructure  if you want to see the digits of pi we  have an api and demos you can play with  at pi.delivery check out the link in the  description below  you can get any numbers between 3.14 and  the 100 trillionth digit 0.  you can also visualize and listen to pi  there  for more record-breaking fun cloud  content subscribe to the google cloud  tech channel  oh you thought i wasn't gonna eat this  pipe  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

